public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.Finiteness
public import MathlibExt.GroupTheory.AmenableGroup

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.FinitelyGeneratedBranchGroupsKourovka15Problem14Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Problem 15.14 (L. Bartholdi, R. I. Grigorchuk, Z.
Šunić); parts (a), (c), (d) in the Archive of Solved Problems, part (b) in the main list.
UnsolvedMath record UM-00701102 (`KOU-15.14`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

Branch groups follow Problem 15.12: a faithful, level-transitive action by automorphisms on a rooted
tree in which every rigid level stabilizer (the subgroup generated by the rigid vertex stabilizers
of the vertices of one level, a rigid vertex stabilizer consisting of the elements whose support
lies in the subtree below the vertex) has finite index. Level-transitivity forces the tree to be
spherically homogeneous, so the tree is modelled as the words whose `i`-th letter is below `m i`,
with all branching indices `m i ≥ 2` as in Grigorchuk's setting (the survey cited in 15.12); tree
automorphisms are the permutations of the vertices preserving the prefix (ancestor) order in both
directions. This is the geometric definition of L. Bartholdi, R. I. Grigorchuk and Z. Šunić, Branch
groups, Handbook of Algebra 3 (2003), arXiv:math/0510294 (Definition 1.13, on the trees of words
with all `m i ≥ 2` of Section 1.2), which their open-problems chapter uses for its versions of
Problems 15.14 (b), (c), 15.15, 15.17 and 15.18 (Questions 20, 15, 14, 10, 9 and 11); Kionke and
Schesler (Section 2.1) and Bondarenko (Section 2) use the same definition. Amenability is
MathlibExt's Følner-condition `IsAmenable`. Growth rates follow Problem 14.7: `ω(G, P) = limsup
β_P(n)^(1/n)`, where `β_P(n)` counts the elements of word length at most `n` for a finite generating
family `P`; the growth type does not depend on the generating set, and the definitions below
quantify over every finite generating family. Containing `F₂` means an injective homomorphism from
the free group of rank `2`.
-/

/-- The vertices of the spherically homogeneous rooted tree with branching sequence `m`: words whose
`i`-th letter is less than `m i`. The length of a word is its level and its prefixes are its
ancestors. -/
def TreeVertex (m : ℕ → ℕ) : Type :=
  {l : List ℕ // ∀ (i : ℕ) (h : i < l.length), l[i] < m i}

/-- The rigid vertex stabilizer of `v` for an action `ρ` on the tree, as a set: the elements whose
support (the set of vertices they move) lies in the subtree rooted at `v`. -/
def rigidVertexStabilizer {m : ℕ → ℕ} {G : Type*} [Group G] (ρ : G →* Equiv.Perm (TreeVertex m))
    (v : TreeVertex m) : Set G :=
  {g | ∀ w : TreeVertex m, ¬ v.1 <+: w.1 → ρ g w = w}

/-- The `n`-th rigid level stabilizer: the subgroup generated by the rigid vertex stabilizers of all
vertices at level `n`. -/
def rigidLevelStabilizer {m : ℕ → ℕ} {G : Type*} [Group G] (ρ : G →* Equiv.Perm (TreeVertex m))
    (n : ℕ) : Subgroup G :=
  Subgroup.closure (⋃ v ∈ {v : TreeVertex m | v.1.length = n}, rigidVertexStabilizer ρ v)

/-- `G` is a branch group (Kourovka 15.12): it acts faithfully and level-transitively by tree
automorphisms on a spherically homogeneous rooted tree with all branching indices at least `2`, and
every rigid level stabilizer has finite index in `G`. -/
def IsBranchGroup (G : Type*) [Group G] : Prop :=
  ∃ (m : ℕ → ℕ) (_ : ∀ i, 2 ≤ m i) (ρ : G →* Equiv.Perm (TreeVertex m)),
    Function.Injective ρ ∧
    (∀ (g : G) (v w : TreeVertex m), v.1 <+: w.1 ↔ (ρ g v).1 <+: (ρ g w).1) ∧
    (∀ v w : TreeVertex m, v.1.length = w.1.length → ∃ g : G, ρ g v = w) ∧
    ∀ n : ℕ, (rigidLevelStabilizer ρ n).FiniteIndex

/-- The growth rate `ω(G, P) = limsup β_P(n)^(1/n)` of a finite generating family `P`, where
`β_P(n)` is the number of elements that are products of at most `n` generators and their
inverses. -/
noncomputable def growthRate {G : Type*} [Group G] {k : ℕ} (P : Group.Generators G (Fin k)) : ℝ :=
  Filter.limsup (fun n : ℕ => (Nat.card {g : G // P.wordLength g ≤ n} : ℝ) ^ ((1 : ℝ) / n))
    Filter.atTop

/-- `G` has exponential growth: it is finitely generated and every finite generating family has
growth rate greater than `1`. -/
def HasExponentialGrowth (G : Type*) [Group G] : Prop :=
  Group.FG G ∧ ∀ (k : ℕ) (P : Group.Generators G (Fin k)), 1 < growthRate P

/-- `G` contains a free subgroup of rank `2`. -/
def ContainsFreeGroupRankTwo (G : Type*) [Group G] : Prop :=
  ∃ f : FreeGroup (Fin 2) →* G, Function.Injective f

/-- Kourovka 15.14(a): there is a finitely generated non-amenable branch group. -/
def fgNonamenableBranchGroup : Prop :=
  ∃ (G : Type) (_ : Group G), Group.FG G ∧ IsBranchGroup G ∧ ¬ IsAmenable G

/-- Kourovka 15.14(b): there is a finitely generated non-amenable branch group without a free
subgroup of rank `2`. -/
def fgNonamenableBranchGroupWithoutFreeSubgroup : Prop :=
  ∃ (G : Type) (_ : Group G), Group.FG G ∧ IsBranchGroup G ∧ ¬ IsAmenable G ∧
    ¬ ContainsFreeGroupRankTwo G

/-- Kourovka 15.14(c): there is a finitely generated branch group containing a free subgroup of
rank `2`. -/
def fgBranchGroupContainingFreeGroup : Prop :=
  ∃ (G : Type) (_ : Group G), Group.FG G ∧ IsBranchGroup G ∧ ContainsFreeGroupRankTwo G

/-- Kourovka 15.14(d): there is a finitely generated branch group of exponential growth. -/
def fgBranchGroupOfExponentialGrowth : Prop :=
  ∃ (G : Type) (_ : Group G), Group.FG G ∧ IsBranchGroup G ∧ HasExponentialGrowth G

/--
Resolved true: the Kourovka Archive answers 15.14(a) "Such groups do exist" (Sidki and Wilson):
their finitely generated branch groups contain free subgroups of rank `2` and so are non-amenable.
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Problem 15.14 (L. Bartholdi, R. I. Grigorchuk, Z.
Šunić); parts (a), (c), (d) in the Archive of Solved Problems, part (b) in the main list;
resolution: S. Sidki and J. S. Wilson, Free subgroups of branch groups, Arch. Math. 80 (2003), no.
5, 458-463 (parts a, c, d); S. Kionke and E. Schesler, Realizing residually finite groups as
subgroups of branch groups, Bull. London Math. Soc. 56 (2024), no. 2, 536-550, arXiv:2212.07165
(part b), Kourovka Archive, Problem 15.14 (a), (c), (d): such groups do exist (Sidki and Wilson's
finitely generated branch groups contain free subgroups of rank 2, the positive answer recorded
under Question 15 of Bartholdi, Grigorchuk and Šunić, Branch groups); Kourovka Notebook (21st
issue), Problem 15.14 (b), and Kionke and Schesler, Corollary 1.2: there is a finitely generated
non-amenable torsion branch group, https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.07165.
-/
public theorem_wanted fgNonamenableBranchGroup_holds : fgNonamenableBranchGroup

